WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.660
In 2009, a mysterious person or persons who
went under the name Satoshi Nakamoto wrote

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:14.370
a piece of software that would become the
foundation of a new kind of money: cryptocurrency.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:19.300
For a while, bitcoin and its brethren was
just a hobby for computer programmers and

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:24.260
math nerds, but recently, it’s drawn the
interest of investors, banks and governments

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:29.270
who wonder if it might actually start competing
with traditional currencies like dollars and

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:30.270
yen.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.960
Bitcoin’s been in the news a lot lately,
but if you’re like most people, you’ve

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.879
probably never used it, and don’t know exactly
what it is.

00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:42.460
But according to some estimates,
over 16 million Americans buy and sell bitcoin

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.450
frequently, and over 100,000 merchants and
vendors worldwide accept it as payment.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.300
But is that enough to qualify as a currency?

00:00:50.300 --> 00:01:03.059
And should you be getting on the crypto-bandwagon
before it’s too late?

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:08.180
In a previous episode, we talked about how
money is based on trust.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.780
You trust that this dollar has value because
your government says it does.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.860
You trust your bank when they tell you how
much money is in your account.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.479
But cryptocurrency is unique in that there
are no centralized powers--no governments,

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:24.909
no banks--to enforce its value or track its
accounting.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:30.119
Instead, every single bitcoin transaction
is added to an encrypted ledger called a “blockchain,”

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:35.000
that is shared publicly on thousands of computers
all over the world.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Updating this blockchain involves solving
a special math problem and takes a lot of

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.000
computer power.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.609
Bitcoin “miners’” who create and update
the blockchain and are rewarded for their

00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:48.280
work with shiny new bitcoins, each imprinted
with the whole history of bitcoin transactions

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.439
since the blockchain began.

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:54.950
It’s as if this dollar bill had a written
record on it of everywhere it’s ever been--and

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.579
everywhere every other dollar bill has ever
been.

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:03.130
No one can counterfeit bitcoin because everyone
knows exactly how much everyone else is supposed

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.130
to have.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:09.009
Your bitcoin “wallet,” doesn’t really
hold bitcoins, it’s just an encrypted key

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:13.610
that gives you access to however many bitcoins
the blockchain says you have.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.909
Which means that, unlike a bank that will
give you a new debit card if you lose yours,

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:20.640
there’s no way to retrieve a lost key.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.251
One man in the U.K. mistakenly threw away
the hard drive that had his key on it, and

00:02:25.251 --> 00:02:31.540
his $100 million worth of bitcoins are permanently
trapped in cyberspace limbo, inaccessible

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.230
to anyone ever.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.230
Bummer.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:39.010
Bitcoin’s not highly regulated, and that’s
made it the go-to currency for shall we say,

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.739
less-than-savory activities.

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:45.920
Drugs, prostitution, and other black-market
items are regularly paid for in bitcoins because

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.070
of the pseudo-anonymity it provides.

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:52.319
According to a study at the University of
Sydney, nearly half of all Bitcoin transactions

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:54.820
are used for illegal activities.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:00.140
And new cryptocurrencies like Monero are growing
in popularity as they claim to be completely

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.140
anonymous.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.090
But there are other reasons why bitcoin hasn’t
gone mainstream.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:08.420
As you’ve probably heard in the news recently,
it’s really volatile.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:14.140
Just in the past couple years, the price of
one bitcoin has gone from around $500 to almost

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.299
$20,000, then back down to around $7,000.

00:03:18.299 --> 00:03:23.220
So even if your local coffee shop wanted to
accept Bitcoin, they’d have to be constantly

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.250
updating the menu board prices.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:28.750
And because it can take pretty long to update
the blockchain, your latte will probably be

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.040
cold by the time you get it.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.909
So, if bitcoin isn’t really feasible as
a mainstream currency just yet… why is everyone

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:37.190
talking about it?

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:43.689
Well, most people are interested in bitcoin
not as a currency, but a speculative investment.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:49.500
Just like gold, tulips or beanie babies, any
resource that fluctuates wildly in price will

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.550
attract people who want to get rich quick.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.000
And some people do.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.909
But speculative investing is ultra-risky.

00:03:55.909 --> 00:04:00.450
Unlike a rental property or a successful business,
bitcoin has no intrinsic value.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.930
It’s a pure gamble, hoping that you can
buy the thing from one sucker and sell it

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.709
to an even bigger sucker for more money.

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:11.409
And even though the number of Bitcoins is
finite--the original programmers coded in

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:16.690
a hard limit of 21 million--new crypto-currencies
using blockchain are popping up every day.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:23.210
Maybe Monero, ZCash or Ethereum will ultimately
overtake Bitcoin as the most popular cryptocurrency.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.240
Some experts even believe that crypto might
become adopted by governments some day.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.100
So, if you’re thinking about investing in
bitcoin, you should ask yourself the same

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.840
things any speculative investor would: Do
I really understand what I’m buying, and

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.620
can I afford to lose my entire investment?

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:42.810
If the answer to either question is no, it’s
probably best to steer clear.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:48.630
Maybe someday we’ll be using cryptocurrency
to rent hoverboards on Mars, but today, it’s

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:52.080
popularity is based more on its potential
than its practicality.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.650
Unless you’re an international criminal,
in which case… cut it out.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:58.580
And that’s our two cents!

